add(
  "english",
  "parts_of_speech",
  "Prepositions",
  `
    <h2>Prepositions</h2>

    <p>A <strong>preposition</strong> expresses a relationship
    between a noun phrase or pronoun and another part of a sentence.
    The relationship may involve time, place, direction, cause
    or other connections.</p>

    <h3>1. Prepositions of place</h3>

    <p>These indicate where something is located.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>The keys are <em>on</em> the table.</li>
      <li>The pupils waited <em>inside</em> the classroom.</li>
      <li>The shop is <em>between</em> the bank and the pharmacy.</li>
      <li>The cat hid <em>under</em> the bed.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Choose the preposition that expresses the intended spatial
    relationship. <em>Under</em> does not mean the same thing as
    <em>beside</em> or <em>behind</em>.</p>

    <h3>2. Prepositions of time</h3>

    <p><em>At</em>, <em>on</em> and <em>in</em> are commonly used
    for different kinds of time expressions.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>At</em> six o'clock; <em>at</em> noon.</li>
      <li><em>On</em> Monday; <em>on</em> 12 June.</li>
      <li><em>In</em> October; <em>in</em> 2026; <em>in</em> the morning.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>These are common patterns, not rules that cover every
    expression. For example, English commonly uses
    <em>at night</em> but <em>in the evening</em>.</p>

    <h3>3. Prepositions of direction and movement</h3>

    <ul>
      <li>The students walked <em>towards</em> the gate.</li>
      <li>The ball rolled <em>into</em> the room.</li>
      <li>The athlete jumped <em>over</em> the hurdle.</li>
      <li>We travelled <em>from</em> Embu <em>to</em> Nairobi.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Notice the difference between <em>in</em> and <em>into</em>.
    <em>In</em> can describe a location; <em>into</em> commonly
    describes movement from outside to inside.</p>

    <h3>4. Prepositional phrases</h3>

    <p>A prepositional phrase begins with a preposition and includes
    its complement, usually a noun phrase or pronoun.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>under the wooden desk</em></li>
      <li><em>after the final lesson</em></li>
      <li><em>with her classmates</em></li>
    </ul>

    <p>In “The bag under the desk belongs to Ali,” the phrase
    <em>under the desk</em> helps identify which bag is meant.</p>

    <h3>5. Prepositions in context</h3>

    <p>Some prepositions form common patterns with particular
    verbs and adjectives.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>interested <em>in</em> science</li>
      <li>depend <em>on</em> someone</li>
      <li>apologise <em>for</em> a mistake</li>
      <li>good <em>at</em> mathematics</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Learn these expressions in meaningful phrases rather than
    selecting a preposition by translating directly from another
    language.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> The visitors arrived at the
    station in the morning and travelled to Meru.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the prepositions:
    <em>at</em>, <em>in</em> and <em>to</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> <em>At</em> connects the arrival
    to a location; <em>in</em> introduces a time expression.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> <em>To</em> expresses the destination
    of the journey.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Each preposition expresses a
    different relationship in the sentence.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      q: "Choose the correct preposition: “The meeting begins ___ 8:30 a.m.” A. in B. at C. on",
      steps: [
        "The expression gives an exact clock time.",
        "English commonly uses “at” before exact times.",
        "The correct phrase is “at 8:30 a.m.”"
      ],
      ans: "at",
      why: "Use “at” with exact clock times."
    },
    {
      q: "Which phrase expresses movement from outside to inside? A. The child is in the house. B. The child walked into the house.",
      steps: [
        "Sentence A describes a location.",
        "Sentence B describes movement towards and across the boundary of the house.",
        "The preposition “into” expresses the movement."
      ],
      ans: "B. The child walked into the house.",
      why: "“Into” expresses movement to the inside of a place."
    },
    {
      q: "Complete the expression: “The success of the project depends ___ careful planning.”",
      steps: [
        "Identify the verb: “depends.”",
        "Recognise the standard expression “depend on.”",
        "Use “on” before “careful planning.”"
      ],
      ans: "on",
      why: "The standard construction is “depend on something.”"
    }
  ]
);


