add(
  "english",
  "nouns_and_noun_phrases",
  "Possessive Nouns",
  `
    <h2>Possessive Nouns</h2>

    <p>A <strong>possessive noun</strong> shows ownership,
    association or a relationship. Apostrophes help mark
    possession in many noun forms.</p>

    <h3>1. Singular nouns</h3>

    <p>For most singular nouns, add an apostrophe and <em>s</em>
    to form the possessive.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>the girl's uniform</li>
      <li>the teacher's desk</li>
      <li>the dog's collar</li>
      <li>James's notebook</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The possessive form of a singular noun generally takes
    <em>'s</em>, including many names that already end in
    <em>s</em>. Some style guides allow alternatives for certain
    names ending in <em>s</em>; follow the convention required
    by your school or publication.</p>

    <h3>2. Regular plural nouns ending in -s</h3>

    <p>When a plural noun already ends in <em>-s</em>, normally
    add only an apostrophe.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>the girls' uniforms</li>
      <li>the teachers' meeting</li>
      <li>the players' changing room</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The apostrophe comes after the final <em>s</em> because
    the noun is already plural.</p>

    <h3>3. Irregular plural nouns</h3>

    <p>Plural nouns that do not end in <em>-s</em> usually form
    the possessive by adding <em>'s</em>.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>children → children's books</li>
      <li>men → men's shoes</li>
      <li>women → women's team</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Distinguishing singular and plural possession</h3>

    <p>Compare:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>The student's books</em> — books belonging to one student.</li>
      <li><em>The students' books</em> — books belonging to multiple students.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The position of the apostrophe changes the meaning.</p>

    <h3>5. Joint and separate possession</h3>

    <p>When two people share one thing, the possessive marker
    usually appears on the last name.</p>

    <blockquote>
      Amina and Brian's project won the competition.
    </blockquote>

    <p>This normally means they worked on one shared project.</p>

    <p>When each person owns a separate item, each name can take
    its own possessive marker.</p>

    <blockquote>
      Amina's and Brian's projects were displayed.
    </blockquote>

    <p>This normally refers to separate projects belonging to
    Amina and Brian respectively.</p>

    <h3>6. Possessive nouns and contractions</h3>

    <p>Do not confuse possession with a contraction.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>The school's gate</em> — the gate belonging to or
      associated with the school.</li>
      <li><em>The school is closed</em> → <em>The school's closed</em>
      — here <em>school's</em> means <em>school is</em>.</li>
      <li><em>It's raining</em> — <em>it's</em> means <em>it is</em>.</li>
      <li><em>The dog wagged its tail</em> — <em>its</em> shows
      possession and has no apostrophe.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Use the meaning and grammar of the sentence to decide
    whether the apostrophe marks possession or missing letters.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> The children's uniforms were
    placed beside the teachers' bags.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> <em>Children</em> is an irregular
    plural that does not end in <em>-s</em>, so its possessive
    form is <em>children's</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> <em>Teachers</em> is a regular
    plural ending in <em>-s</em>, so its possessive form is
    <em>teachers'</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Both forms are correct because
    their plural nouns follow different spelling patterns.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      q: "Choose the correct form for a notebook belonging to one student. A. the students notebook B. the student's notebook C. the students' notebook",
      steps: [
        "The notebook belongs to one student.",
        "A singular noun usually forms the possessive with apostrophe + s.",
        "Write “the student's notebook.”"
      ],
      ans: "the student's notebook",
      why: "The singular possessive form is “student's.”"
    },
    {
      q: "Complete the phrase for bags belonging to several teachers: “the teachers___ bags.”",
      steps: [
        "The noun “teachers” is already plural and ends in “s.”",
        "For a regular plural noun ending in “s,” add an apostrophe after the final “s.”",
        "The correct phrase is “the teachers' bags.”"
      ],
      ans: "the teachers' bags",
      why: "The apostrophe follows the final “s” in a regular plural possessive."
    },
    {
      q: "Choose the correct sentence: A. The dog wagged it's tail. B. The dog wagged its tail.",
      steps: [
        "The sentence describes a tail belonging to the dog.",
        "The possessive determiner “its” shows ownership without an apostrophe.",
        "“It's” normally means “it is” or “it has.”"
      ],
      ans: "B. The dog wagged its tail.",
      why: "“Its” is possessive; “it's” is a contraction."
    }
  ]
);
