// 17. Future Forms
add(
  "english",
  "tenses_and_aspect",
  "Future Forms",
  `
    <h2>Future Forms</h2>

    <p>English uses several structures to refer to future events. The best choice depends on whether the speaker is expressing a prediction, an intention, a plan, an arrangement or a timetable.</p>

    <h3>1. Will + base verb</h3>
    <p>Use <strong>will + base verb</strong> for predictions, decisions made at the time of speaking, offers and promises.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>I think our team <strong>will win</strong>.</li>
      <li>The phone is ringing. I <strong>will answer</strong> it.</li>
      <li>I <strong>will help</strong> you carry those books.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Be going to + base verb</h3>
    <p>Use <strong>am/is/are going to + base verb</strong> for prior intentions and predictions based on present evidence.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>We <strong>are going to revise</strong> together this evening.</li>
      <li>Look at those dark clouds. It <strong>is going to rain</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Present continuous for arrangements</h3>
    <p>Use the present continuous for planned personal arrangements, especially when the time or details have been agreed.</p>
    <p><em>I am meeting the principal at ten tomorrow.</em></p>

    <h3>4. Present simple for timetables</h3>
    <p>The present simple often describes scheduled events.</p>
    <p><em>The examination begins at eight tomorrow morning.</em></p>

    <h3>5. Future continuous</h3>
    <p>Use <strong>will be + verb-ing</strong> for an activity that will be in progress at a future time.</p>
    <p><em>At this time tomorrow, we will be sitting our examination.</em></p>

    <h3>6. Future perfect</h3>
    <p>Use <strong>will have + past participle</strong> for something expected to be completed before a future point.</p>
    <p><em>By Friday, she will have completed the project.</em></p>

    <h3>7. Common errors</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Incorrect: <em>She will goes home.</em></li>
      <li>Correct: <em>She will go home.</em></li>
      <li>Incorrect: <em>They are going to studying.</em></li>
      <li>Correct: <em>They are going to study.</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to choose a future form</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the intended meaning: prediction, intention, arrangement, schedule or activity in progress.</li>
      <li>Choose the structure that expresses that meaning.</li>
      <li>Use the correct verb form after the auxiliary or phrase.</li>
    </ol>
  `,
  [
    {
      q: "You have just noticed that a classmate has dropped several books. Choose the most natural offer.",
      options: [
        "I will help you pick them up.",
        "I am helped you pick them up.",
        "I will helping you pick them up."
      ],
      steps: [
        "The offer is being made at the moment of speaking.",
        "Use 'will' for a decision or offer made at that moment.",
        "Follow 'will' with the base verb 'help'."
      ],
      ans: "I will help you pick them up.",
      why: "'Will + base verb' is a natural structure for an immediate offer."
    },
    {
      q: "The school has arranged a meeting with parents for tomorrow at ten. Which sentence best expresses that arrangement?",
      options: [
        "The school meets the parents yesterday.",
        "The school is meeting the parents tomorrow at ten.",
        "The school has met the parents tomorrow at ten."
      ],
      steps: [
        "The meeting is a planned arrangement.",
        "The present continuous can express an arranged future event.",
        "Use 'is meeting' with the singular subject 'school'."
      ],
      ans: "The school is meeting the parents tomorrow at ten.",
      why: "The present continuous can express a confirmed future arrangement."
    },
    {
      q: "Complete the sentence: 'By the end of this week, I ___ the assignment.'",
      options: [
        "will have completed",
        "will completing",
        "have completed tomorrow"
      ],
      steps: [
        "'By the end of this week' sets a future deadline.",
        "The sentence focuses on completion before that future point.",
        "Use 'will have' followed by the past participle 'completed'."
      ],
      ans: "will have completed",
      why: "The future perfect expresses an action expected to be complete by a specified future time."
    }
  ]
);

// 18. Choosing the Correct Tense
add(
  "english",
  "tenses_and_aspect",
  "Choosing the Correct Tense",
  `
    <h2>Choosing the Correct Tense</h2>

    <p>Choosing a tense is not just about spotting a time word. Consider when the event happens, whether it is complete or ongoing, and how it relates to another event or to the present.</p>

    <h3>1. Identify the time reference</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Habit or general truth:</strong> The earth revolves around the Sun.</li>
      <li><strong>Action in progress now:</strong> The learners are writing.</li>
      <li><strong>Completed past event:</strong> The learners wrote yesterday.</li>
      <li><strong>Ongoing past action:</strong> They were writing when the bell rang.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Decide what the sentence emphasises</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Present perfect simple:</strong> She has completed three exercises. — completed result.</li>
      <li><strong>Present perfect continuous:</strong> She has been practising for an hour. — activity and duration.</li>
      <li><strong>Past perfect:</strong> She had left before I arrived. — an earlier past event.</li>
      <li><strong>Past perfect continuous:</strong> She had been waiting for an hour when I arrived. — duration before a past point.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Check the time expression</h3>
    <p>Time expressions provide clues, but context determines the best tense.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>every day</em> often signals a routine.</li>
      <li><em>right now</em> often signals an action in progress.</li>
      <li><em>yesterday</em> normally points to the past simple for a completed event.</li>
      <li><em>since Monday</em> or <em>for two hours</em> can accompany perfect forms when the situation connects to a reference point.</li>
      <li><em>by Friday</em> may indicate completion before a future deadline.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Keep the time relationship clear</h3>
    <p>Compare:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>When I arrived, the lesson started.</em> — the arrival and start are presented as past events in sequence.</li>
      <li><em>When I arrived, the lesson had started.</em> — the lesson started before my arrival.</li>
      <li><em>When I arrived, the lesson was starting.</em> — the lesson was in the process of starting.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Maintain consistency without making tense changes mechanical</h3>
    <p>In a narrative, the tense may change when the time reference changes. A change is not automatically an error.</p>
    <p><em>Yesterday, we visited the museum. Today, we are writing a report about it.</em></p>

    <h3>A practical decision process</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Locate the time reference.</li>
      <li>Decide whether the action is habitual, ongoing, completed or earlier than another event.</li>
      <li>Choose the tense that expresses that relationship.</li>
      <li>Check subject agreement and the verb form required by the tense.</li>
    </ol>
  `,
  [
    {
      q: "Choose the correct sentence for a routine.",
      options: [
        "He is going to school every weekday.",
        "He goes to school every weekday.",
        "He has gone to school every weekday yesterday."
      ],
      steps: [
        "'Every weekday' describes a regular routine.",
        "The present simple is normally used for routines.",
        "The third-person singular subject 'he' takes 'goes'."
      ],
      ans: "He goes to school every weekday.",
      why: "The present simple expresses repeated habits and routines."
    },
    {
      q: "The teacher arrived at 9 a.m. The lesson started at 8:30 a.m. Choose the sentence that correctly shows the lesson started first.",
      options: [
        "When the teacher arrived, the lesson had started.",
        "When the teacher arrived, the lesson has started.",
        "When the teacher arrived, the lesson will start."
      ],
      steps: [
        "Both events occurred in the past.",
        "The lesson began before the teacher arrived.",
        "Use the past perfect for the earlier event."
      ],
      ans: "When the teacher arrived, the lesson had started.",
      why: "'Had started' places the beginning of the lesson before the teacher's arrival."
    },
    {
      q: "Choose the best tense: 'The learners ___ their assignment now, so please do not disturb them.'",
      options: [
        "complete",
        "are completing",
        "had completed"
      ],
      steps: [
        "'Now' and the request not to disturb them indicate an activity in progress.",
        "Use the present continuous.",
        "The plural subject 'learners' takes 'are'."
      ],
      ans: "are completing",
      why: "The present continuous describes an activity in progress at the present time."
    }
  ]
);
